WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.640
 Hello and welcome to chapter four, Functions.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:06.240
 This is the fourth of our basic patterns.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.560
 We'll get to iterations next.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.640
 Functions is the store and reuse.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.300
 One of the things in programming is that

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.200
 we never like to repeat ourselves.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.720
 We don't like to, if we have four or five lines of code

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.640
 and we're gonna do the same thing later,

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.600
 we don't like to put the same four lines of code in.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.280
 Even if, it has to do with reliability.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.440
 If you find something wrong with those four lines of code

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:33.280
 and you've got them 12 different places in your program,

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.120
 then you gotta find all 12 places and fix 'em.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.600
 So we're like, collect those to one place

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.560
 and then call them and reuse them.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.560
 And that's the idea of store and reuse.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.000
 So this is how functions work inside of Python.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.940
 The first thing we notice is there is a new keyword, def.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.360
 That stands for define function.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.840
 And the def is like an if statement

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.800
 or we'll see fours and whiles that they end in a colon

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.360
 and then they have an indented block

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.160
 and then the indented block deindents

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.360
 and that's the end of the function.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.360
 And so there's two statements make up this function.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.560
 The key thing that you have to understand and get used to

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.460
 is this def part is actually not running any code whatsoever.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.200
 It's actually remembering the code.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.080
 And that's what I call the store phase.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.080
 The def creates a bit of code and records it like a macro,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.820
 although it's much more complex than a macro.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.480
 And it names it whatever you chose.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.440
 You gave it a name.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.180
 We named this one thing.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.540
 And so it has a side effect of Python reading

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.840
 or parsing these three lines.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.680
 It doesn't do anything but it remembers.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.200
 These two lines are what you would like to run

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.140
 when you invoke thing.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.160
 So this is the definition of a function

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.560
 and this is the invoking of the function.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.440
 But, so this doesn't do anything.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.260
 So there's no output here from that stuff right there.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.680
 But then what happens is you invoke it.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.160
 And this thing looks like it's part of Python,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.240
 but you have an effective extended Python

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.060
 with your def statement.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.900
 And so when it sees thing, it goes up and runs your code.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.080
 And so out comes hello fun.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.400
 And then it comes back and goes to the next line.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.760
 Does print, so print comes out.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.880
 And then it goes back and like oh, this is the reuse part.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:21.780
 But we get to reuse it.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.560
 We define it once and we use it twice.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.140
 Then it runs this code again and it goes to the next line

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:26.980
 and it's all done.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.300
 So this little bit came out twice.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.660
 And of course this is really simple

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.720
 so that I can fit it on a page.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.640
 But you get the idea that I don't want to repeat.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.000
 This might be 15 to 100 lines of code

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.280
 and I don't want to type those over and over again.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.280
 So I say hey, store these in a name that I choose.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.480
 And then when I invoke them,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.160
 bring them back and then run them again.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.360
 Okay, so that's the basic idea.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.040
 We actually have already been using functions

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:56.880
 from the beginning.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.540
 The print is a function, right?

00:02:58.540 --> 00:02:59.760
 Print is a function.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.840
 Every time we see print, P-R-I-N-T parenthesis

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.420
 and then we have some stuff in here,

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.200
 we are calling the print function.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.680
 This is the syntax with two little parentheses

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.420
 is the syntax for functions.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.820
 And so inputs a function, type is a function,

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.240
 floats a function, ints a function.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.520
 All these things are built in functions

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.520
 that come with Python at the moment that we started.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.240
 I mean we installed Python and these came along.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:33.480
 And then there's other functions that we define and use

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.320
 and that's what the def is for.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.280
 And in effect we can create new reserved words

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.160
 of our own making that extend the Python language

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.480
 after we define the function.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.640
 So it's just this bit of reusable code

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:48.840
 that takes some arguments.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.000
 We haven't seen any with arguments.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:50.920
 There's a little parenthesis

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.680
 and we'll see how that works in a bit.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.280
 We define using the def keyword and then we invoke it.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.560
 There's the defining phase

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:58.640
 which actually doesn't run the code,

00:03:58.640 --> 00:03:59.640
 it just remembers the code.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.480
 And then there's the invoking phase.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.640
 You define it once and then invoke it one or more times.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.200
 Calling the function or invoking the function,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.800
 we think of those two things as the same thing.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.920
 Call, invoke, or just the terms we use.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.760
 Most people just say call the function.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.340
 But invoking it is a perhaps more descriptive way

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.580
 to think about it.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:21.340
 So here's an example of a function.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.480
 It is built into Python.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.120
 It's called the max function.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.280
 And we can pass some parameters into the max function.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.480
 So we pass the hello world string.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.000
 Now, like much of Python,

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.520
 max knows what kind of thing is being passed into it

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.860
 and it knows that it's looking for the largest character,

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:42.720
 the lexicographically largest character.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.040
 And in this case, it scans this little,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.440
 that's inside the max code.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.720
 It scans through and finds the largest character.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.360
 So apparently, lower case letters are higher

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.360
 than upper case letters because in English,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.660
 we get back a W.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:58.520
 And so this is what's called the return value.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.840
 So this is an assignment statement.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.780
 Let me clear this and start over.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:04.500
 So this is an assignment statement.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.000
 So it has to evaluate this right-hand side.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.280
 And a function call is nothing more than like X plus one.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.000
 It's something to evaluate.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.920
 It runs the function code, passes in this argument.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:16.640
 And then this residual value,

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:17.880
 this is called return value.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.680
 We'll look at this in more detail.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:23.840
 Becomes the result of this little bit in the expression

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:25.000
 and there's nothing else.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.480
 We could have W plus one or something.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.940
 And then the W is what's stored into big.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.760
 Okay, so we print big and big is a variable

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:36.480
 that has the letter W inside of it.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.520
 And then we ask what is the smallest?

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.780
 And that finds the blank.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.440
 And so we get a blank to see this.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.560
 There's a min function and a max function.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.260
 Both of these are built in.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:48.520
 These are built in functions.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:49.960
 They're always there for us.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:57.000
 Okay, so here is another example of the max function.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.040
 And so we can think of this as invoking

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.240
 or calling this function

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.580
 as this right-hand side is being evaluated.

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:06.360
 We are passing this variable in

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:07.840
 and there's some code in here.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:10.280
 And it's gonna do some stuff, yada, yada, yada.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:14.200
 And then it's gonna give us back a bit of stuff.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.120
 And that's its return value.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.120
 And then that goes up into the big, right?

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.240
 And so that's how this works.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:23.600
 And so this is actually built in.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:27.360
 Built in or burnt in, I guess.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:28.200
 Can't draw.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.200
 And so you can think of this as some time, a long time ago,

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:34.600
 when Python was being first formed,

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:36.300
 somebody wrote some code.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.760
 And it's got some stuff in it.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.720
 It's got a little loop that reads through all the,

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:40.720
 reads through all the letters.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.960
 It has to figure out if it's a string or a list,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.300
 et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.760
 But this is store,

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:49.400
 except you didn't do the storing

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:50.360
 'cause it's already built in.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:53.060
 And then this is the reuse, store and reuse.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.820
 So we build these things into Python.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:56.460
 They're already pre-built.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.000
 As if before the first line of your code executes,

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:02.760
 way up here, someone put all this code in for you

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.200
 into Python and created a thing called max for you.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.340
 Now we've been using this already, built in functions.

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:11.900
 We've got type conversions.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:15.400
 We've got like the float that takes a integer

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.940
 and returns a floating point version of that.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:21.480
 And again, this is kind of like an expression.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.960
 So it's like, I wanna divide this by 100,

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.640
 but before I do that, I've gotta convert it to a float.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.760
 So it has to sort of do these function calls

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.280
 as it's evaluating the expression, okay?

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:38.760
 Sometimes, like here, we just have a,

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:40.360
 prints out the return value.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:41.200
 That's what this is.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:42.280
 This is the return value.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.600
 If you just type a function in a parameter,

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:47.080
 it can be in a constant or it can be a variable.

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:48.120
 And as we'll see in a second,

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.200
 we'll give you many of these if you like.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:54.000
 So you can either just run it or take the result of this.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.920
 This passes an integer in, converts it to a float,

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:59.600
 and then puts the float into that.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.520
 Type tells us what kind of thing that is,

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:03.880
 and you can use this inside of an expression.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:05.640
 And so it's like, what am I gonna do first?

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.640
 Oh, I gotta do two times this thing.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:10.840
 Oh, wait a sec, pause just briefly for a moment,

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:15.420
 call out to some float code, pass a three into it,

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:18.840
 and then something comes back, the return value,

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.260
 the residual value comes back, and then that participates.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:24.460
 In this case, it's gonna be 3.0,

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.280
 participates in this two times 3.0, okay?

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:31.520
 And so two times 3.0 ends up being 6.0, et cetera, et cetera.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.800
 But you can see as it, it's like, oh, wait a sec,

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:35.800
 I gotta figure out what this is, call the function,

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:36.640
 get the return value,

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:39.520
 and then continue processing this expression.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.920
 We've also done this with string conversions,

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.680
 partly because, just as an example,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:48.320
 the input always returns a string,

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.440
 the input function returns a string.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.000
 And so, you know, here's this string,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:56.760
 could be coming from input, but we'll just take one, two, three.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:59.940
 We know that that's a string, it's not the number 123.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:03.060
 And if we try to add one to it, we get a trace back,

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:08.060
 cannot concatenate string and integer, trace back.

